Description: antinomie: record the system calls of launched and attached processes
 Record what a program does, from inside the guest kernel, without
 ptrace, a tracer process or anything the program can see. For each
 system call of a traced task the recorder publishes an Entry event with
 the arguments and the input memory they point to, and an Exit event
 with the result and the output memory. Lifecycle events say when the
 program was launched (with its path, argv and environment), forked a
 child, completed an exec and exited. Every event is a sequence of
 records in the per-CPU telemetry rings: a 144-byte header (stream, task
 cookie, call id, event and packet sequence, timestamps, pid and tid,
 CPU, launch cookie and capture cookie) and the fields, each sent in
 chunks of at most 4 KiB.
 .
 Memory is read without faulting. A field the recorder could not read
 says why: copy fault, invalid extent, no decoder, resource exhausted,
 lost to a full ring, or not resident (the range is mapped and readable
 but not yet in memory, such as a forked child's literals). An input
 that was not resident at entry is read again at the end of the call
 and published with the Exit, marked as recaptured; after a successful
 exec the exec's own copy of the path and the new image's argument and
 environment strings stand in for the caller's memory. Failures and
 losses are counted (STATS) and marked in the events they affect.
 .
 Two scopes are traced. A program the host launched, with every
 descendant: the launch tags the task with a cookie that its children
 inherit. And capture sessions, for processes the host did not start:
 PROCESS_LIST pages through the guest's processes, CAPTURE_ATTACH traces
 one process and its future descendants, CAPTURE_ALL every user process,
 and CAPTURE_STOP and CAPTURE_STATUS end and query a session. One
 session is active at a time; capture ids are never reused within a
 boot and the 64 most recent stay addressable.
 .
 The calls the recorder traces, their argument counts and the names of
 undecoded pointer arguments come from the system call table. The
 common calls have built-in decoders (read, write, readv, writev, open,
 openat, stat, fstat, unlink, rename, execve, execveat, connect, bind,
 accept, sendto, recvfrom, clone and terminal ioctls); the next patch
 adds the decoders for the other families.
 .
 Hooked kernel files:
 - arch/x86/entry/common.c calls the recorder at system call entry and
   exit, the only places that see every native call with its registers.
 - kernel/fork.c clears the task fields in dup_task_struct(), tags a
   traced parent's child under tasklist_lock before it can run (so a
   capture session cannot miss a thread), publishes the fork after it,
   and frees the task's context with the task.
 - fs/exec.c records the exec commit, and keeps the exec's copy of the
   path for a recapture.
 - kernel/exit.c records the task's exit.
 - include/linux/sched.h adds the task's cookies, its recorder context
   and whether it is counted.
 .
 Contract: telemetry records are application protocol 1, header minor 2
 (the capture cookie at offset 136). Capture sessions came with
 revisions 11 and 12; the recapture marks (FIELD_BEGIN flags RECAPTURED
 and KERNEL_COPY) are additive within revision 17, and a host that does
 not read them sees the fields as before.
